  "amendments": [
    "batch_size 1 for knob 4 against the frozen 16. Motivated by the padding measurement (notebook 21 cell 6) before any eviction EM existed. Verified: evict_none_b1 vs frozen baseline b=0 c=1 d=1, 998/1000 predictions string-identical, EM 0.3920 vs 0.3910.",
    "RoPE position-shifting ON for all non-contiguous policies, with evict_sink4_r050_naive as a single-budget ablation.",
    "GQA aggregation MEAN over the 8 query heads per KV head, plus a forced MEAN over the 2 KV heads (index_select on the sequence dim applies across heads, so the keep-set is per-layer). Pre-registered, not tuned.",
    "Attention policy defers eviction to the first decode step: the scoring hook fires after the update() it would inform, so no scores exist at prefill. Position policies evict at prefill. Budgets match from step 1.",
    "ADDED random and attention at keep_ratio 0.75 and 0.25. The original list gave position policies three ratios and control policies one, so the headline claims each rested on a single budget. Oversight in the setting list, not a design decision; r=0.50 results were already seen when the gap was noticed. Adds x-axis coverage only \u2014 same metric, same acceptance criterion, same reference. It changed the conclusion: random reverses from best policy at r=0.75 to worst at r=0.25."
  ],
  "predictions": {
    "efficiency_null": {
      "registered": "Eviction imposes a measurable quality cost against an efficiency benefit that does not materialise at this scale, because eviction acts after prefill and peak memory occurs during prefill.",
      "outcome": "CONFIRMED (smoke, notebook 26): peak memory 6.11-6.15 GiB across all settings against 5.87 GiB of weights. Evicting 75% of the cache moved peak by 0.02 GiB (0.3%)."
    },
    "rope_shift_small": {
      "registered": "RoPE position-shifting matters LESS at ~744 tokens than in the long-context literature, where holes span millions.",
      "outcome": "CONFIRMED. Paired shifted vs naive: zero discordant, identical marginals (EM 0.2820 both). 32/1000 predictions changed, none changed correctness."
    },
    "attention_beats_position": {
      "registered": "Attention-based eviction beats position-based at equal keep_ratio, because gold evidence sits mid-prompt where recency drops it and sink+recent hollows it out.",
      "outcome": "CONFIRMED at every keep_ratio (see attn_vs_sink_* tests)."
    },
    "attention_beats_random": {
      "registered": "Implied by the above: content-aware selection should separate from a coin flip.",
      "outcome": "SEE mcnemar. At r=0.50 the separation was an adequately powered NULL (b=58 c=41 d=99 p=0.1074) \u2014 recorded as a falsification when r=0.50 was the only budget measured. The amendment adding r=0.75 and r=0.25 changed the picture: random is BEST at r=0.75 (0.378) and COLLAPSES at r=0.25 (0.087, below every other policy). The separation is budget-dependent, not absent. Final wording set from the r=0.25 test rather than from r=0.50 alone."
    }
  },
